    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Micro Frontends** is an enterprise performance-architecture concern. Explain the ownership boundary, how it affects delivery/runtime performance, and which governance or measurement mechanism keeps the system maintainable.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nIdentify the user-visible symptom or engineering bottleneck, define the source of truth, change one variable, and measure the result before adding more complexity.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThis version exposes the loop, condition, state transition, or calculation so you can explain the logic line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Service {\n  name: string;\n  owner: string;\n}\n\nfunction ownerOf(\n  services: readonly Service[],\n  name: string,\n): string | null {\n  for (let i = 0; i < services.length; i += 1) {\n    if (services[i].name === name) {\n      return services[i].owner;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\nconst owner = ownerOf(\n  [{ name: \"checkout\", owner: \"payments\" }],\n  \"checkout\",\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform helpers when they make the same behavior clearer and the underlying performance trade-off is already understood.\n\n```ts\nconst ownership = new Map([\n  [\"checkout\", \"payments\"],\n]);\n\nconst owner =\n  ownership.get(\"checkout\") ?? null;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse Micro Frontends only when the organizational or architectural boundary justifies the added operational cost. Pair it with ownership, budgets, observability, and rollback.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and measurement:\nExplain the dominant cost, then validate it with the appropriate tool—build analysis, browser trace, RUM, network waterfall, memory evidence, or mobile-device testing.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nTest cold and warm paths, cache misses, failures, slow networks, low-end devices, repeated interactions, large inputs, and rollback/recovery behavior where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nState the bottleneck, evidence, implementation choice, trade-off, and the guardrail that prevents the regression from returning.",
